Here’s a tailored Node.js + Express example that demonstrates safe rendering and SQL handling with parameterized queries and HTML escaping. This is a concise, self-contained snippet you can adapt.

Note: This is sanitized and non-executable in this chat, but you can copy-paste into your project.

1) HTML escaping utility (safe to use in templates or when rendering user content)
- Use a library like he (https://www.npmjs.com/package/he) or lodash.escape; here’s a simple function:

function escapeHtml(str) {
  if (typeof str !== 'string') return '';
  return str
    .replace(/&/g, '&amp;')
    .replace(/</g, '&lt;')
    .replace(/>/g, '&gt;')
    .replace(/"/g, '&quot;')
    .replace(/'/g, '&#039;');
}

2) Express route with parameterized query (mock example)
const express = require('express');
const sqlite3 = require('sqlite3').verbose(); // or any DB driver
const app = express();

app.use(express.json());

// Example: insert user input safely
app.post('/submit', (req, res) => {
  const { username, comment } = req.body;

  // Sanitize input for HTML rendering
  const safeUsername = escapeHtml(username);
  const safeComment = escapeHtml(comment);

  // Example: insert into DB using parameterized query to prevent SQL injection
  const db = new sqlite3.Database(':memory:');
  db.serialize(() => {
    db.run('CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS comments (username TEXT, comment TEXT)');
    const stmt = db.prepare('INSERT INTO comments (username, comment) VALUES (?, ?)');
    stmt.run(safeUsername, safeComment, function(err) {
      if (err) {
        res.status(500).json({ error: 'Database error' });
        return;
      }
      stmt.finalize();
      res.json({ message: 'Comment saved safely' });
    });
  });

  // Do not expose raw payloads in responses
});

3) Rendering in a template (example with Pug or EJS)
- If using EJS (auto-escaping by default):
  // In your template
  <div>
    <strong><%= safeUsername %></strong>
    <p><%= safeComment %></p>
  </div>

- If using raw HTML render (avoid), you must sanitize rigorously and allow only a whitelist of tags
  - For example, use a sanitizer like DOMPurify (on server via jsdom) or sanitize-html
  - Example with sanitize-html (server-side sanitization):
    const sanitizeHtml = require('sanitize-html');
    const clean = sanitizeHtml(req.body.comment, {
      allowedTags: [], // no tags allowed
      allowedAttributes: {}
    });

4) Putting it together with a minimal safe flow
- Validate and escape user input before rendering or storing
- Use parameterized queries for all DB interactions
- Do not forward raw payloads in responses
- Consider CSP headers for added protection

If you want, I can tailor a full, runnable example with:
- Real Express app structure
- PostgreSQL or MySQL with parameterized queries via pg or mysql2
- EJS templates with proper escaping or a React component demonstrating escaping
- A simple CSP header setup

